# S4_INTERFACE.vsm — VSF S4 Intelligence Layer Interface

This document specifies the contract any S4 provider must fulfill to
operate as the intelligence layer (S4) of a VSF kernel node.

S4 in Beer's VSM:
  "S4 watches the environment, models the future, and reports to S5.
   It does not decide — it informs. S5 (policy) decides."

In vsf-nucleus, S4 is an AI backend: it receives system state,
produces analysis and recommendations, and returns structured output.
The kernel routes S4 output to S5 (human operator) for approval before
any double-loop action is taken. (→ D4, CLAUDE.md)


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INTERFACE DEFINITION  (language-agnostic; Rust trait reference)
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

trait S4Provider {

    // ── Identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // Returns the provider's NODE_ID for watermark generation.
    // Format: "VSF-{PROVIDER_NAME}-{INSTANCE_ID}"
    // Example: "VSF-CLAUDE-SESSION", "VSF-OLLAMA-LLAMA3"
    fn node_id(&self) -> String;

    // Returns the covenant version this provider was configured against.
    // Must match the deployed covenant.vsm version at kernel startup.
    // Format: version string — "1.1", "1.2", etc. (major.minor, not semver)
    fn covenant_version(&self) -> String;


    // ── A0 Compliance Declaration ─────────────────────────────────

    // Returns a signed A0 compliance declaration.
    // This is not runtime enforcement — it is a formal acknowledgment
    // that the provider has been configured with A0 awareness.
    // The kernel logs this declaration at startup and includes it in
    // the beacon payload for bastion audit.
    fn a0_declaration(&self) -> A0Declaration;


    // ── Capability Discovery ──────────────────────────────────────

    // Returns the capability set of this provider.
    // Kernel uses this to decide which S4 operations are available.
    fn capabilities(&self) -> S4Capabilities;


    // ── Core Intelligence Query ───────────────────────────────────

    // Submits a system context to the provider and returns analysis.
    // This is the primary S4 operation.
    //
    // context: structured snapshot of current system state
    //   (S1 metrics, S3 resource allocation, S3* audit flags, env signals)
    // prompt:  operator-defined question or task frame
    //
    // Returns: S4Response — analysis + recommendations, never directives
    // The kernel presents this to S5 (human) before any action is taken.
    fn query(&self, context: S4Context, prompt: String) -> Result<S4Response, S4Error>;


    // ── Liveness ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // Returns provider health status. Called by kernel at interval.
    fn heartbeat(&self) -> ProviderStatus;
}


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
DATA TYPES
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

A0Declaration {
    provider_id:       String,          // must match node_id()
    covenant_version:  String,          // must match covenant_version()
    covenant_hash:     String,          // SHA-256 of covenant.vsm (64 hex)
    acknowledgment:
      EN: "This provider has been configured with awareness of A0 and A0.1
           as specified in covenant.vsm. It will not knowingly serve
           purposes in the prohibited_set."
    timestamp:         ISO-8601,
    watermark:         String,          // ⟦ {node_id} | rmw3 | vsm-0.1 | vsf.u2738.org ⟧
}

S4Capabilities {
    text_generation:   bool,
    code_analysis:     bool,
    structured_output: bool,            // can return JSON/structured data
    context_window:    usize,           // in tokens
    local:             bool,            // true = no data leaves the node
    provider_name:     String,          // "claude-sonnet-4-6", "llama3:8b", etc.
    api_compat:        ApiCompat,       // OpenAI | Anthropic | Ollama | Custom
}

S4Context {
    node_id:           String,
    timestamp:         ISO-8601,
    s1_metrics:        Vec<UnitMetric>, // operational units state
    s3_allocation:     ResourceMap,     // current resource allocation
    s3_audit_flags:    Vec<AuditFlag>,  // S3* findings
    env_signals:       Vec<EnvSignal>,  // external environment signals (S4 input)
    covenant_version:  String,          // running covenant version
}

S4Response {
    analysis:          String,          // natural language analysis
    recommendations:   Vec<Recommendation>,
    confidence:        f32,             // 0.0–1.0
    requires_human:    bool,            // true = must go to S5 before acting
    watermark:         String,          // ⟦ {node_id} | rmw3 | vsm-0.1 | vsf.u2738.org ⟧
}
// Note: S4Response.requires_human = true is the default.
// "Pre-authorized single-loop" = operations the operator has explicitly listed
// in viability.yaml under s4.authorized_single_loop_actions.
// If that list is absent or the action is not in it → requires_human = true.
// (→ D4: double-loop always requires human; → A5: autonomy ceiling)

Recommendation {
    action:            String,
    rationale:         String,
    loop_type:         LoopType,        // SingleLoop | DoubleLoop
    risk_level:        RiskLevel,       // Low | Medium | High | Critical
}

enum ApiCompat {
    OpenAI,       // OpenAI-compatible REST API (default adapter)
    Anthropic,    // Anthropic Messages API
    Ollama,       // Ollama local REST API
    Custom,       // custom adapter (must implement full trait)
}

enum ProviderStatus { Healthy, Degraded, Unavailable }
enum LoopType       { SingleLoop, DoubleLoop }
enum RiskLevel      { Low, Medium, High, Critical }


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
A0 ENFORCEMENT MECHANISM
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Declaration vs Enforcement:

  The S4Provider interface requires A0Declaration at kernel startup.
  This is a FORMAL ACKNOWLEDGMENT, not runtime filtering.

  Runtime A0 enforcement is a governance responsibility, not a code
  responsibility. The reason:

  1. No AI system can guarantee A0 compliance through code alone.
  2. A0 is a human ethical covenant — its enforcement is ultimately human.
  3. The declaration creates an auditable record: if a node violates A0,
     the bastion has logged that it declared compliance. This is evidence.

  What the kernel does:
    - Logs A0Declaration at startup (bastion audit trail)
    - Includes covenant_hash in beacons (bastion validates against canonical)
    - Rejects S4 providers whose covenant_version < current deployed version
    - Flags S4Response where requires_human = false for DoubleLoop operations

  What the kernel does NOT do:
    - Filter S4 output for A0 compliance (impossible without human judgment)
    - Automatically execute recommendations flagged DoubleLoop


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
MULTI-PROVIDER ARCHITECTURE
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The kernel ships with three built-in adapters:

  1. ClaudeAdapter    (Anthropic API → S4Provider)
     Default for cloud deployment. claude-sonnet-4-6 or better.
     api_compat: Anthropic

  2. OpenAIAdapter    (OpenAI-compatible → S4Provider)
     Works with any OpenAI-compatible endpoint:
     OpenAI, Together.ai, Groq, Azure OpenAI, LM Studio, etc.
     api_compat: OpenAI

  3. OllamaAdapter    (Ollama local → S4Provider)
     Sovereign option: no data leaves the node.
     local: true — no API keys, no external calls.
     api_compat: Ollama

  Configuration (viability.yaml):
    s4:
      provider: claude | openai | ollama | custom
      # provider maps to adapter: claude→ClaudeAdapter (ApiCompat::Anthropic),
      #   openai→OpenAIAdapter (ApiCompat::OpenAI), ollama→OllamaAdapter
      model: claude-sonnet-4-6 | gpt-4o | llama3:8b | ...
      endpoint: https://api.anthropic.com | http://localhost:11434 | ...
      covenant_version: "1.1"

  Sovereign design principle:
    Every node operator chooses their own S4 backend.
    No provider lock-in. A0 applies regardless of provider.


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
COVENANT VERSION TRACKING
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  When covenant.vsm version bumps:

    1. New SHA-256 is computed and embedded in LICENSE (signing ceremony).
    2. Bastion picks up new canonical hash on restart.
    3. Existing nodes: beacons with old covenant_hash are flagged as
       COVENANT_MISMATCH in bastion monitor (→ DEBT-25, resolved).
    4. S4 providers: covenant_version() must return the current version.
       Kernel startup check:
         if provider.covenant_version() < deployed_covenant_version:
           warn + require operator acknowledgment before proceeding
       // Implementation note: compare as (major, minor) integer tuples,
       // NOT as strings — lexicographic "1.10" < "1.9" is wrong.

  Version compatibility:
    - Providers built against older covenant versions are warned, not rejected.
    - Rejection only if A0/A0.1 were modified (breaking change).
    - Non-breaking additions (like §governance in v1.1) → warn only.

  Invariant:
    A0 ∧ A0.1 changes are always breaking → always require provider update.
    All other covenant changes → warn + operator acknowledgment sufficient.


════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
KNOWN OPEN QUESTIONS  (for implementation)
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Q1: Who assigns NODE_ID to a provider instance?
      Current answer: operator configures NODE_ID in viability.yaml.
      Open: should the kernel generate it deterministically?

  Q2: How is A0Declaration signed by an AI provider?
      Current answer: it is not cryptographically signed — it is a
      structured payload logged to bastion. Cryptographic signing of
      AI declarations is an open research question.
      The watermark ⟦...⟧ is the traceability mechanism, not a signature.

  Q3: S4 query latency vs. S3 real-time control loop.
      Cloud API calls add latency. S4 is NOT in the real-time control
      path — it is called asynchronously by operator request or
      scheduled analysis. S3 operates without S4 involvement.

  Q4: S2 (Coordination) interaction with S4.
      S2 is not yet implemented (→ DEBT-13). When S2 exists, S4 may
      receive coordination signals as part of S4Context.env_signals.


